WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.020
OMG

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:15.080
You might think that the lights above my head are stars, but they can't be because I'm in

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.840
a cave.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:23.200
Each one of those tiny lights is produced by the larva of a small insect called a fungus

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:30.160
gnat as a way of attracting its prey.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.880
The result is a display that must surely rank as one of the most magical illuminations in

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:43.680
the whole of the natural world.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.120
But shine the light sideways across the ceiling.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.440
Then you can see that each little blue lamp is surrounded by a curtain of glistening,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:00.400
beaded filaments, curtains that are invisible at other times.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:08.680
They're lures and they can be lethal.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:16.080
Insects hatching in the water below fly up towards these tiny lights and here they are

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:24.520
trapped by threads of this extraordinary material that is the unique possession of the invertebrates.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:39.800
This is silk.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:47.160
This astonishing cave is near the small town of Waitomo in New Zealand.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:53.220
Each light comes from the back end of a larva as it lies in a transparent tube of mucus

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.400
slung from the ceiling by silk and threads.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.580
And it's produced by phosphorescent chemicals in a special compartment opening from the

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:06.220
side of its intestine.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:12.560
The silk comes from glands at the other end, inside the larva's mouth.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.560
The larvae move around.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.880
They fix a silk thread to the rock and slowly inch their way over the ceiling along a network

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.680
of threads.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.480
Arriving in a new position, the larva produces more silk, but this time it allows the thread

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:38.000
to dangle downwards.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:51.360
As each section emerges from its mouth, the larva with a gulp adds a blob of glue.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:04:00.240
Eventually a single strand may be a metre long.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.800
There can be several hundred larvae in a single square metre of cave roof.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:09.640
And they all work hard producing strand after strand.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:15.480
The more they make, the greater their chances of catching something.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.720
Below mayfly are hatching from the stream that runs through the cave.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:24.520
They've been carried in here by the current from outside as larvae.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.880
Now they must look for a mate.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:33.640
But they find the blue lights above irresistible.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:43.480
And they're caught.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:49.400
The fungus gnat detects its victim's struggles from lines that run between the threads.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.720
Having made a capture, it turns off its light.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.000
That saves energy.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.800
Laboriously, it makes its way across to the thread from which the vibrations are coming.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.320
It holds it up and eats what's hanging on the end.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.480
It also eats the filament.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:15.160
That saves silk.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:22.160
This wonderful hunting technique is just one of an enormous number of varied ways in which

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.440
animals use silk.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.160
Silk really is an extraordinary material.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.640
It's stronger than a steel thread of the same diameter.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.480
And unlike steel, it's elastic.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:43.760
It can stretch up to twice its length.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.160
The inhabitants of the undergrowth developed the ability to produce this marvelous material

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:55.120
very early in their evolutionary history, over 300 million years ago.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:02.200
At first, it seems, they used it in a very simple way, as an adhesive.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:09.680
And lacewings still do, though for them, it's an adhesive with a difference.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.340
This is a female.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.560
She is looking for a safe place to deposit her eggs.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:22.760
Silk will provide it, but not exactly in the way you might think.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:27.760
She will lay up to 300 eggs, almost twice her body weight.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:34.360
However, there are plenty of other insects around that will eat those eggs if they find

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.760
them.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:43.200
So she doesn't glue them directly on the plant's stem.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.520
First, she produces a little drop of sticky silk.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:59.080
And then, at the end of that, the egg.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:05.920
It's suspended safely in midair.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:10.720
The silk is produced by glands in her abdomen in liquid form.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.120
It's the very act of pulling it out that changes it from liquid to solid.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:25.920
And that is true for all invertebrate silk.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:35.400
She will lay up to 30 eggs a day, each on its own stalk.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:41.680
But the silk and thread is so incredibly fine that insect predators like these ants walk

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:49.480
right by the eggs without realizing that there's a tasty meal within millimeters of them.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:58.320
So despite regular ant patrols in search of food, the lacewing's eggs remain undiscovered.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:05.280
After three days, they begin to hatch.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:11.120
Now at least if danger threatens, her offspring will be able to help themselves by running

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:17.800
away.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:26.360
In the lush rainforest of Trinidad, you can find sheets of silk wrapped around trees.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:35.600
Here it's also used for protection, but by a quite different creature in a quite different

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:36.600
way.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:50.660
The manufacturers, a little-known group of insects called web spinners, live beneath.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:57.400
They graze on algae and lichens hidden by the sheets immediately above them.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:03.400
They produce their silk not from their abdomens or their mouths, but from glands in their

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:19.720
forelegs.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:41.280
Each leg has about 150 tiny silk ejectors, which between them create a thin silken tissue.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:10:00.240
An ant in search of prey strolls over the surface of the web spinner's marquee.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.480
But the silk sheet, thin though it is, is impervious to smells, and as long as the web

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:16.600
spinner doesn't move too much, the ant will be unaware of it, a millimetre beneath its

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:17.600
feet.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:24.720
And the tent, like any decent tent, is waterproof.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.840
In fact, the tent is so waterproof that the web spinners beneath are in danger of not

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.560
getting enough water.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:38.280
So after the storm is over, they bite holes in places where the little rain has accumulated

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:54.060
and drink the tiny puddle dry.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:58.800
Of course, the hole has to be repaired after a drink, but that's easy enough when you

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:06.240
have an almost limitless supply of silk in your legs.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:10.660
Of all the inhabitants of the undergrowth that have exploited silk, none have done so

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:17.080
with more variety and skill than the spiders, and this is almost certainly the first way

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:20.640
in which they used it.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:27.440
Here on this bank in the Malaysian rainforest, there are strands of silk radiating from this

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:29.920
little patch in the middle.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:36.280
Watch what happens if I touch one of them.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:45.800
I can't help jumping.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:51.400
That was a trapdoor spider, but it was so swift that you hardly saw it.

00:11:51.400 --> 00:12:00.000
Let's see if I can get it to do it again.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.600
The spider, when hungry, sits close behind the trapdoor.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:09.320
The strands outside are all connected to a silken collar that surrounds the mouth of

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:11.800
the hole.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:14.420
Each of her feet is in contact with it.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:24.000
The slightest twitch is enough to tell her that something's moving around outside.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.040
A single twitch will produce no reaction.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:32.360
That could be caused by a falling leaf or a drop of water, but a repeated vibration,

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:38.000
especially if it moves from one strand to another, could mean prey.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:45.040
I like this beetle.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:52.720
Got it.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:56.220
Now it'll kill it.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.480
This is the most ancient of living spiders.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:05.840
The fact that it has uniquely segmented plates on its back shows that it's more closely related

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:09.960
than any other to those pioneer hunters, the scorpions.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.960
And like them, it has a powerful venom.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:20.320
Once bitten, its victim has little chance.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:26.000
Triplines were one of the earliest of the spiders' hunting techniques, but other, later

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:34.520
spiders used silk to build much more sophisticated structures.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:40.280
Orb webs are so familiar to us that we tend to forget what complex structures they are.

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:47.240
A single one can contain up to 60 meters of silk of up to six different kinds and involve

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:51.040
3,000 separate attachments.

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:58.360
And what's more, some orb web spiders spin a different one every night.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:02.560
The biggest and best webs are made in most species by the female.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:07.580
She has to start by bridging the gap across which she's to hang her web.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:13.000
The faintest breeze will catch a filament as she spins it and carry it away into space.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:19.680
With luck, it will catch on a suitable anchor point.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.280
There.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:26.360
She runs across the filament, trailing a line of much thicker, stronger silk, and ties it

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:28.320
off.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:36.040
Then she goes back to the middle of this line and drops down another.

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:50.120
And she tightens it.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:54.800
The junction at the top becomes the hub of the web to which she will attach radiating

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.400
spokes.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:16.000
These must be particularly strong, for the shape of the whole structure depends on them.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:25.200
Once they're complete, she adds a spiral, working from the middle outwards.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:30.320
This first spiral is quite widely spaced because it's only temporary.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:35.240
It will serve as a scaffolding along which she runs to add a stronger, stickier, and

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:38.840
more closely spaced spiral.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:43.160
That's this one.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:48.720
As the filament for this emerges from her spinneret, she coats it with glue from separate glands

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:52.520
in her abdomen.

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:56.320
After completing one section, she eats the scaffolding line.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:02.160
It has no further use, and it saves valuable silk.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:07.840
At first, this glue is evenly spread, but each time she fixes a section, she twangs

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:25.520
it with her leg so that it breaks up and forms a line of droplets.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:41.160
She can complete the whole intricate, elegantly symmetrical structure in about an hour.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:17:03.400
When an insect strikes the web, the capture spiral stretches and then retracts to its

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:08.560
former size, without distorting its shape and without such a severe recoil that the

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:13.000
insect might be catapulted off again.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:16.040
The beads of glue are the key.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:22.320
Tension on the surface of a droplet hauls any slack into it.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:28.480
When the insect hits, it pulls out the coils of thread in each droplet, slowing the insect

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:32.680
to a momentary standstill.

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:40.680
And then the surface tension pulls the silk back into each drop.

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:49.400
So the spiral thread doesn't break, and the web as a whole regains its symmetry.

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:54.600
The spider sits with her legs resting on the spokes.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:18:00.600
Any vibration on them will travel up her leg and be received by a small sense organ in

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:02.920
the joint.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:08.360
This is covered with microscopic slits which are distorted by the slightest movement.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:12.560
So the spider is immediately aware of the tiniest tremor.

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:18.600
Once alerted, she pulls on neighbouring spokes of the web to assess exactly in what direction

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:33.160
and how far away the signals originate.

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:36.760
The fly is on the verge of breaking loose.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:39.400
Here she comes.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:44.120
She isn't hindered by the glue she put on the capture spiral because her feet are coated

00:18:44.120 --> 00:19:02.600
with a special oil.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:07.600
Once her victim is in her grasp, she produces yet another kind of silk.

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:11.640
It emerges as a sheet from a group of minute spigots.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:32.520
This is a fuzzy silk that's used for wrapping and at moments like this as a shroud.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:39.720
The biggest and strongest webs are those made by Nephila, the golden orb web spider of the

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:40.720
tropics.

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:48.360
They're several metres across and they're strong enough to catch small birds.

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:53.080
This time only a moth.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:59.480
After a killing bite, she returns to the hub of her web to wrap it up.

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:02.240
But big webs bring problems.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:07.400
It's not easy to control what happens on their outer regions.

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.600
This is Archeroides.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:15.600
She's only one hundredth the weight of Nephila, so she can move across this huge web undetected

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:19.040
and she's a thief.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:23.600
A fly has arrived not far from her.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:30.560
She has a chance to steal it.

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:53.040
But Nephila has also detected its arrival and claims it without much difficulty.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:57.080
Another fly is caught in the web.

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:03.080
Archeroides now stands a better chance since Nephila is busy feeding.

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:08.200
She cuts the filaments between the fly and Nephila so that vibrations made by its struggles

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:32.520
won't reach her.

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:37.800
Nephila sitting at the hub of the web seems quite unaware of what's going on at its outer

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.200
margin.

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:45.600
The fly is now hanging from a single thread, but it's five times the weight of Archeroides

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:47.620
and too heavy for her to carry.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:49.880
She has to be clever.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:22:03.360
She attaches a thread to it and runs it up to a twig outside the web.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:09.280
Nephila is still occupied with her meal.

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:25.920
Another line just to make sure.

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:46.200
Now she can snip the last filaments of the web and haul it away.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:23:00.200
Safely off Nephila's web at last, Archeroides can enjoy her stolen meal in safety.

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:06.040
For all its complexity, the orb web was one of the first kind of silken traps devised

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:07.520
by spiders.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:14.440
Subsequently, other species modified it in some quite extraordinary ways.

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:20.000
There's a web in this yew tree that's triangular, a slice as it were from an orb.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:26.160
It's made by Hippotetes and her body forms an essential link in its mooring cable.

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:32.000
To be effective, the web has to be very taut.

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:45.520
Hippotetes ratchets up the tension by hauling in the main cable and coiling it above her

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:48.520
body.

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.520
Tighter.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:54.320
Tighter.

00:23:54.320 --> 00:24:03.080
And that's about as tight as it'll go.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:05.760
Now she has to wait.

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:10.400
Flies can sometimes disentangle themselves from a web if the spider doesn't grab them

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:18.600
quickly, but a fly hitting this web won't get that chance.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:23.040
A strike triggers an instant reaction.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:26.120
In slow motion, you can see what happens.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:32.080
Hippotetes immediately lets go of the coil she was holding over her back.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:42.920
That causes her web to collapse and almost instantaneously entangle the prey.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:52.100
Few flies that hit her Hippotetes web managed to escape.

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:58.720
The gladiator spider makes her web from a very special kind of multi-strand silk which

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:01.840
she back cones to make fuzzy.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:14.960
She carefully attaches this to a framework of ordinary, unfuzzy filaments.

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:23.760
The fuzzy silk doesn't have glue on it, but it will entangle hairy legs.

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:44.000
And it's also extremely elastic, which is crucially important.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:45.920
She's finished.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:53.520
She reaches down with her forelegs to check how far away she is from the ground.

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:58.720
Then she snips most of the framework threads and holds the fuzzy rectangle between her

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:00.800
fore front legs.

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:05.600
She's ready.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:11.720
Her enormous eyes are so sensitive she can hunt in near darkness.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:16.560
A bush cricket would make a rich meal, but it's very powerful and it could put up a good

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:39.280
fight.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:45.000
Now it must be parceled up and the fuzzy silk makes excellent wrapping, just as it

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:51.880
does for Hippotetes.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:57.520
In Australia, there's a species of spider that has taken web construction a stage further

00:26:57.520 --> 00:26:58.520
still.

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:02.100
It builds not just in two dimensions, but three.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:07.160
It regularly takes up residence in people's backyards and on their verandas.

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:09.680
This one under this plant holder.

00:27:09.680 --> 00:27:12.280
It's notorious and very venomous.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:13.280
Red back.

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:20.400
And what's brought it here is the extraordinary way in which it uses silk.

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:25.640
The female usually builds at night and constructs this very elaborate web.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:29.360
It's not just wide, it's deep.

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:33.520
To make it, she needs two flat surfaces, one beneath the other.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:38.000
And that's what she's found underneath the plant holder.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:43.740
First she drops down, pulling a thread behind her.

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:50.960
She sticks the end to the veranda floor.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:57.720
Then she goes back up again, trailing a second line, which she sticks to the first, so strengthening

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:00.260
it.

00:28:00.260 --> 00:28:02.620
Then she pulls the line tight.

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:14.120
That is a crucial element in the construction.

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:38.880
Down she goes again.

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:44.420
By the time she's finished, she will have fixed several dozen of these sticky, taut,

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:47.980
vertical lines.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:50.580
An ant is approaching in the distance.

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:55.780
An orb web would never catch one of these.

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:59.480
It's a scout leading an exploring party.

00:28:59.480 --> 00:29:03.840
Searching beneath the plant holder, it's almost bound to blunder into one of the redback's

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.700
lines.

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:13.740
It struggles and so seals its fate.

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:26.540
And its followers go the same way.

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:32.380
The threads carry the vibrations back to the redback, waiting above.

00:29:32.380 --> 00:29:34.220
She has no need to hurry.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:36.740
Her meals are suspended in midair.

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:38.620
Escape is impossible.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:49.740
She hauls them up in her own good time.

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:55.300
The redback's trap is certainly economical with silk, but one North American spider hunts

00:29:55.300 --> 00:29:59.040
with just a single filament.

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:04.460
This may look like a bird dropping, but that's just as this guy is to fool anything that

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:05.820
might want to eat it.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:12.420
In fact, it's a spider, and one with an even more extraordinary hunting technique.

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:15.060
It's a bolas spider.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:18.060
Throughout the day, she remains motionless.

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:27.540
But when evening comes, she prepares for action.

00:30:27.540 --> 00:30:36.260
She abandons her disguise and starts to move.

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:44.980
Slowly she makes her way down to the underside of the leaf.

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:52.340
There she hangs from a horizontal thread.

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:57.900
Next she starts to spin a single strong line, pulling it out of her spinneret with her back

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:07.300
legs.

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:11.900
And at the end, there is a sticky globule.

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:13.700
This is her bolas.

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:15.780
It's all she needs.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:21.340
She climbs back up to her leaf and takes up her position on the horizontal thread with

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:41.260
her weighted filament dangling from one of her front legs.

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:42.260
A moth.

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:45.620
She whirls her bolas, but misses.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:49.860
But she has ways of enticing the moth back.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:55.340
She can produce a pheromone, a chemical perfume, that the moth finds irresistible.

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:59.860
What is more, she can change it to suit the particular species of moth that happens to

00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:03.900
be around.

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:10.380
The moth comes back.

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:23.060
This time she's got it.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:33.100
Now she starts to wrap it.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:40.420
But she's not finished yet.

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:53.620
Different moths and a different pheromone.

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:56.420
Silk can do other things as well.

00:32:56.420 --> 00:33:02.940
It can totally change a spider's lifestyle and turn a solitary killer into a creature

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:05.500
that hunts in great packs.

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:10.980
This enormous web above me contains thousands of spiders.

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:19.940
They're all tiny, but because they work together, they can kill prey many times their own size.

00:33:19.940 --> 00:33:24.860
Any spider sitting on its web might be expected to react aggressively towards another that

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:26.580
approaches it.

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:32.740
But not these.

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:37.180
These tiny ant-sized spiders seem totally relaxed in one another's presence.

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:42.900
More than that, they cooperate with one another, working together to repair and extend their

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:46.540
huge silken palace.

00:33:46.540 --> 00:34:03.380
There are tens of thousands of them in this one, and they are constantly at work.

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:09.940
Their home can rise 15, 20 metres up towards the canopy.

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:17.420
And so big, it's a major obstacle in the airways of the forest.

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:21.860
This cricket weighs several hundred times as much as one of these spiders.

00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:27.140
However, the slightest attempt to free itself only serves to attract lots of them from all

00:34:27.140 --> 00:34:47.060
over the giant web.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:50.860
Soon it's surrounded by hundreds.

00:34:50.860 --> 00:35:06.620
They squirt glue from their scrinnerettes, immobilising the cricket limb by limb.

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:34.940
They sink their tiny jaws into its most vulnerable places, its joints, and inject their venom.

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:57.300
Not long the cricket is dead, and the horde of tiny victors share their vast meal.

00:35:57.300 --> 00:36:01.420
On occasion, even a solitary spider must meet another spider.

00:36:01.420 --> 00:36:04.940
A male, after all, must meet female.

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:11.380
This is a male, Argyriopy, and he is looking for a mate.

00:36:11.380 --> 00:36:14.700
But she is huge, ten times bigger than he is.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:27.020
He has to be very careful if he's not to be mistaken for prey and eaten.

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:32.420
Once he reaches her, he starts stroking her body, nibbling her toes.

00:36:32.420 --> 00:36:36.140
From their taste, he can tell whether the female is a virgin.

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:42.300
If she is, she'll be less likely to eat him.

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:48.420
To confirm that her taste is encouraging, he wipes his feet across his mouth.

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:55.920
Apparently, he's reassured, for he starts to snip some of the strands of her web to create

00:36:55.920 --> 00:37:01.500
an open space in it.

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:06.820
He runs a line across it, down towards her.

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:25.100
And now he plucks it like a guitar string.

00:37:25.100 --> 00:37:26.860
He's doing very well.

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:39.260
She's not attacked him yet.

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:41.340
She spreads her eight legs.

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:46.600
It's a clear invitation to mate.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:53.260
He checks the taste on his legs again and decides to go further.

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:54.860
He pauses.

00:37:54.860 --> 00:37:59.900
After mating, he has at best a 50-50 chance of staying alive.

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:02.720
But nothing ventured, nothing gained.

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:09.380
He moves in and delivers his sperm.

00:38:09.380 --> 00:38:11.780
But his luck runs out.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:38.540
Then she may be, but with mating completed, she grabs him and binds him in silk.

00:38:38.540 --> 00:38:44.780
We will eat him later.

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:51.340
Some spiders don't spin webs of any kind, but they still need silk to help them find

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:52.340
a mate.

00:38:52.340 --> 00:38:55.620
And there's one such just here.

00:38:55.620 --> 00:39:03.420
It's a female wolf spider, a solitary wandering hunter.

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:08.660
Like all spiders, she trails a drag line of silk behind her wherever she goes.

00:39:08.660 --> 00:39:13.740
It's a safety line in case she falls or is blown away or needs to drop out of sight in

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:15.660
a hurry.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:22.900
And here's a male.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:24.680
He's noticed her drag line.

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:28.240
The taste of a silk line is very informative for him, too.

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:35.940
He tells him that it comes from a female, so he follows.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:43.720
His black palps are covered in hairs which are extremely sensitive.

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:51.940
Each hair contains a nerve which can detect even minute quantities of female pheromone.

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:54.560
Now he's within sight of her.

00:39:54.560 --> 00:40:00.780
Among active hunters, wolf spiders have excellent eyesight, so he uses his black palps to send

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:12.900
visual signals to her.

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:15.500
This display is not slowed down.

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:17.400
This is how he does it.

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:39.140
It takes a lot of energy, and while he's performing, his heartbeat triples.

00:40:39.140 --> 00:40:53.820
She encourages him by tapping her legs.

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:56.380
He's now within striking distance.

00:40:56.380 --> 00:41:08.880
The palps he's waving, like those of all male spiders, are loaded with sperm.

00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:32.140
He leans over, inserts one of them into her abdomen, and pumps his sperm into her.

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:37.120
Then he does the same with the other.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:43.660
And that's that, at any rate, as far as he is concerned.

00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:48.100
Three weeks pass, and the female's ovaries start to produce eggs.

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:52.400
The male's sperm that the female has been holding within her for all this time is now

00:41:52.400 --> 00:41:55.180
released and fertilises them.

00:41:55.180 --> 00:41:59.200
At last, she's ready to lay.

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:04.300
But she needs a safe place in which to do so.

00:42:04.300 --> 00:42:09.660
And once again, silk provides a solution to her problems.

00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:23.120
She starts by spinning a silken sheet stretched between fragments of the leaf litter.

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:27.300
She uses that fuzzy silk that comes from multiple nozzles.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:50.820
And to provide a soft padding to protect her eggs.

00:42:50.820 --> 00:43:00.220
She expels a drop of liquid onto the sheet.

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:27.700
And into the liquid, she injects her fertilised eggs.

00:43:27.700 --> 00:43:30.960
There may be several dozen of them.

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:41.780
She checks that the drop has dried.

00:43:41.780 --> 00:43:52.820
She adds more fuzzy silk to protect it and its vulnerable contents from knocks and bumps.

00:43:52.820 --> 00:44:21.180
Then she changes silk and starts to spin a tougher kind to cover the whole capsule.

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:26.980
She cuts the platform free from its attachments and goes round it, pinching the cut edges

00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:34.060
firmly together.

00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:59.340
Finally, she covers the whole parcel with a waterproof silk and wrapping.

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:08.180
She now carries her precious package with her wherever she goes.

00:45:08.180 --> 00:45:12.980
She seeks out patches of sunlight so that she can warm it and speed the development

00:45:12.980 --> 00:45:17.300
of the eggs within.

00:45:17.300 --> 00:45:26.900
It's a long process that may last several weeks.

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:54.740
And then, at last, her babies are sufficiently developed to leave their nursery.

00:45:54.740 --> 00:45:59.660
But even now, she doesn't abandon them.

00:45:59.660 --> 00:46:03.420
They climb up her legs and onto her back.

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:11.500
The egg capsule is now empty and can be discarded.

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:14.060
And away they go.

00:46:14.060 --> 00:46:18.860
It's a somewhat rough ride, but the babies, even at this early stage in their lives, know

00:46:18.860 --> 00:46:21.900
how silk can keep them out of trouble.

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:35.540
They use it to tie themselves to their mother's back.

00:46:35.540 --> 00:46:41.060
And then they use it for yet another purpose and produce it in such abundance that in some

00:46:41.060 --> 00:46:54.060
seasons of the year it covers great areas of the open countryside.

00:46:54.060 --> 00:47:02.660
This wonderful shimmering carpet of gossamer, strands of the finest silk, is the creation

00:47:02.660 --> 00:47:05.660
of a million baby spiders.

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:16.260
It's autumn in England and time for spiderlings to leave their mothers.

00:47:16.260 --> 00:47:25.700
The youngsters climb up the threads they've spun to reach the topmost twigs of the bushes.

00:47:25.700 --> 00:47:30.740
They tip their abdomens into the air and the gentle breeze catches the filaments as they

00:47:30.740 --> 00:47:36.220
issue from the spinnerets.

00:47:36.220 --> 00:47:53.060
Some filaments drift down and become entangled in the bushes.

00:47:53.060 --> 00:48:03.260
But when conditions are right, the threads rise vertically upwards.

00:48:03.260 --> 00:48:18.780
And away the spiderlings go.

00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:22.980
On a calm day, they may only travel a few metres.

00:48:22.980 --> 00:48:30.380
But if there's a breeze, as there is now, they can be swept up high into the sky.

00:48:30.380 --> 00:48:42.100
Spiderlings have been recorded thousands of feet up and can travel for hundreds of miles.

00:48:42.100 --> 00:48:49.340
So silk can be used for transport as well as looking after the young, courtship and

00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:51.740
of course catching prey.

00:48:51.740 --> 00:48:58.020
In an area of heath like this around me, it's been estimated that there's probably 14,000

00:48:58.020 --> 00:49:03.460
miles of silk, enough to stretch from here in England to Australia.

00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:09.340
Ingenious though we are, we've not yet been able to invent anything as strong, as light

00:49:09.340 --> 00:49:27.100
or as elastic as silk.

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:32.340
In the next programme of Life in the Undergrowth, we explore a fascinating world of intimate

00:49:32.340 --> 00:49:38.380
relations.

00:49:38.380 --> 00:49:45.980
Ants with aphids, aphids with plants, ladybirds with ants, aphids and plants.

00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:50.340
Because invertebrates have existed for such a long time, they have evolved relationships

00:49:50.340 --> 00:49:52.860
so complex they're almost unbelievable.

00:49:52.860 --> 00:49:59.340
For us, trying to unravel the true story of what's going on was a real problem.

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:06.660
First, we went to the foremost scientists and got the very latest chapter in their research.

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:11.340
And then it was a question of developing the technology to try and capture such intricate

00:50:11.340 --> 00:50:13.700
behaviour.

00:50:13.700 --> 00:50:20.540
To get really close to our subjects, but without disturbing what they're doing.

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:26.900
Martin Dawn is fine tuning a portable motion control rig that can position tiny lenses

00:50:26.900 --> 00:50:35.860
to within millimetres, so he can spy into the world of intimate relations from a distance.

00:50:35.860 --> 00:50:41.340
You get a lovely sense of motion, a sense of tracking, you can, you know, flying in

00:50:41.340 --> 00:50:46.620
a helicopter at the moment just over these extraordinary gigantic plants.

00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:56.380
It's just given me and hopefully others a new perspective on the world.

00:50:56.380 --> 00:51:02.580
And it was exactly that new perspective that we needed in Peru to look into a bizarre relationship

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:08.500
between an ant and a plant.

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:13.980
For two years, scientist Megan Fredrickson has been investigating some strange clearings

00:51:13.980 --> 00:51:15.500
in the forest.

00:51:15.500 --> 00:51:20.980
She discovered that they're actually made by ants, tiny ants which diligently weed out

00:51:20.980 --> 00:51:29.540
all types of plants except this one, which they protect and in return get a home.

00:51:29.540 --> 00:51:35.100
What Megan doesn't know is exactly how the ants are killing the rifle plants.

00:51:35.100 --> 00:51:38.020
And that's what Martin is hoping to help her find out.

00:51:38.020 --> 00:51:49.660
So he sets up his rig in the clearing.

00:51:49.660 --> 00:51:53.460
It's not a case that the ants are going out and they're actually cutting off the leaves

00:51:53.460 --> 00:51:55.940
of the plants that are trying to grow here.

00:51:55.940 --> 00:52:01.620
They seem to be attacking the leaves using some kind of chemical that they produce.

00:52:01.620 --> 00:52:06.420
Megan thinks the ants are poisoning the plants they don't want, but because they're so small,

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:12.020
she can't see how they're managing it.

00:52:12.020 --> 00:52:17.420
But with Martin's array of microscopic lenses, she can look into the ants' tiny world in

00:52:17.420 --> 00:52:19.820
detail.

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:26.940
And what she sees is quite remarkable.

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:29.740
They may be small, but that doesn't stop them.

00:52:29.740 --> 00:52:33.380
Their first action is to cut through the plant's protective bark.

00:52:33.380 --> 00:52:38.140
Then they swing their abdomens under their legs and squirt a drop of lethal formic acid

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:45.660
into the wound.

00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:51.100
Ants are minuscule amounts, but then there may be over five million ants in one clearing,

00:52:51.100 --> 00:52:53.580
so it soon adds up.

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:59.300
At last, Megan can see how her ants are poisoning plants.

00:52:59.300 --> 00:53:10.300
And we get some quite extraordinary footage.

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:15.580
Just as we've used technology to get us that insect eye view, Chris Watson has used all

00:53:15.580 --> 00:53:35.780
kinds of devices to eavesdrop on the extraordinary sounds of the undergrowth.

00:53:35.780 --> 00:53:41.060
And there's one sound in particular that could play a key role in another very startling

00:53:41.060 --> 00:53:43.860
relationship.

00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:50.940
Jeremy Thomas has spent over 30 years studying one caterpillar, the larva of the blue butterfly,

00:53:50.940 --> 00:53:53.580
which starts life on a plant.

00:53:53.580 --> 00:53:57.940
The caterpillar feeds for about two or three weeks on the plant, and when it's still very

00:53:57.940 --> 00:54:00.180
tiny, it falls off the plant.

00:54:00.180 --> 00:54:07.460
And at that stage, it produces some secretions that mimic one species of red ant's chemicals.

00:54:07.460 --> 00:54:13.300
And so it's so similar to the ant's own grub that the ant mistakes it for its ant grub,

00:54:13.300 --> 00:54:18.980
picks it up in its jaws, runs underground into its nests, and actually places the caterpillar

00:54:18.980 --> 00:54:21.060
with its own ant brood.

00:54:21.060 --> 00:54:26.420
And from then on, the caterpillar actually induces the ants to feed it instead of their

00:54:26.420 --> 00:54:30.260
own grubs.

00:54:30.260 --> 00:54:34.620
But that's not the end of it, because it has recently been discovered that the caterpillars

00:54:34.620 --> 00:54:41.700
also produce sounds, and Jeremy thinks they're important.

00:54:41.700 --> 00:54:47.080
The problem is that they are incredibly quiet, which is where Chris comes in with a new bit

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:48.080
of kit.

00:54:48.080 --> 00:54:54.380
We're using very special microphones, particle velocity microphones, which are being built

00:54:54.380 --> 00:54:58.260
into this soundproof container.

00:54:58.260 --> 00:55:03.500
And the caterpillars are introduced onto the very element of this special microphone.

00:55:03.500 --> 00:55:11.480
And we've had to come into a BBC radio studio here, simply because we need somewhere with

00:55:11.480 --> 00:55:15.180
very little, if any, background sound.

00:55:15.180 --> 00:55:20.300
Because the sounds produced are so quiet, so minimal, that we would never be able to

00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:24.940
hear them, let alone record them outside.

00:55:24.940 --> 00:55:31.420
Try not to breathe too much.

00:55:31.420 --> 00:55:40.780
It's doing it now.

00:55:40.780 --> 00:56:03.260
Some of these sounds have been heard before, though never this clearly, but others are

00:56:03.260 --> 00:56:04.260
completely new.

00:56:04.260 --> 00:56:12.580
It was such a revelation, truly, really an amazing thing to hear it for the first time.

00:56:12.580 --> 00:56:29.140
It's probably the quietest sound I've ever had the opportunity of recording.

00:56:29.140 --> 00:56:34.860
Apart from the alarm sounds of the caterpillar, which arouses the ants and brings them scurrying

00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:40.620
to the caterpillar, presumably to protect it, we simply don't know what the other noises

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:41.720
do.

00:56:41.720 --> 00:56:48.180
So what we're going to do is take the tapes from the BBC and play them back to the ants

00:56:48.180 --> 00:56:51.820
and just watch what the response of the ants is.

00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:52.820
Does it make them fight?

00:56:52.820 --> 00:56:53.820
Does it make them run away?

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:54.820
Does it make them run towards the sound?

00:56:54.820 --> 00:56:55.820
Does it make them bring food to the sound?

00:56:55.820 --> 00:56:56.820
Anything is possible.

00:56:56.820 --> 00:57:07.620
We simply don't know until we try.

00:57:07.620 --> 00:57:12.660
Whatever happens, we know that this caterpillar is the most extraordinary survivor.

00:57:12.660 --> 00:57:16.980
Nurtured and protected by the ants for up to two years, the little grub that made those

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:41.980
strange noises eventually emerges as a beautiful adult butterfly.

00:57:41.980 --> 00:57:58.700
A numbers rocks.
